    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across devices. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices within a layer. Every device participates in every layer.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models, where different devices own *different experts* (entire FFN blocks), and tokens are routed to whichever device holds the relevant expert.\n\nIn practice, large MoE deployments combine both: attention layers use TP, MoE layers use EP (often with TP inside each expert), and **data parallelism (DP)** may be layered on top for replicated copies. This note covers the mechanics of TP and EP inference, their data flow, the collectives involved, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention: $Y = \\text{softmax}(QK^T/\\sqrt{d})V \\cdot W_O$\n- MLP: $Y = \\sigma(XW_1)W_2$\n\nThe canonical Megatron-style sharding:\n\n| Component | Sharding | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| $W_Q, W_K, W_V$ | Column-parallel (split output dim) | Attention heads are independent; splitting by head gives each rank a disjoint set of heads |\n| $W_O$ | Row-parallel (split input dim) | Each rank's partial $V$ output multiplies its slice of $W_O$ |\n| $W_1$ (up/gate) | Column-parallel | Activations split along hidden dim; GELU/SiLU are elementwise |\n| $W_",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
